/* generated by build_lessons.py from the Episode 1 audio script */
(function(r,f){if(typeof module==='object'&&module.exports)module.exports=f();else r.LessonData=f();})(typeof self!=='undefined'?self:this,function(){return {
 "balance": [
  "Let's start with the balance sheet.",
  "A balance sheet is a photograph. It is taken on one single day, like December thirty-first. It shows three things. Assets, liabilities, and equity.",
  "Assets are the things the company owns or controls that have value. Cash in the bank. Money customers owe the company. That is called accounts receivable. Inventory sitting on the shelf. And long-term things like equipment, buildings, and aircraft.",
  "Liabilities are what the company owes to other people. Bills it has not paid yet, which are called accounts payable. Wages it owes employees. Loans from the bank.",
  "Equity is what is left over for the owners. It is the owners' stake.",
  "Now there is one more distinction you need. Current versus long-term. Current means it turns into cash, or comes due, within one year. Long-term means more than a year. So cash and receivables are current assets. An aircraft is a long-term asset. The bill you pay next month is a current liability. The twenty-year mortgage is a long-term liability. Notice why this matters.",
  "If you want to know whether a company can survive the next twelve months, you compare current assets to current liabilities. Keep that thought, because it comes back when we do ratios.",
  "Here is the balance sheet for Skyline Avionics on December thirty-first.",
  "Current assets. Cash, fifty thousand dollars. Accounts receivable, thirty thousand. Inventory, twenty thousand. That makes total current assets one hundred thousand dollars.",
  "Long-term assets. Equipment and building, net of depreciation, three hundred thousand dollars. I will explain depreciation in a minute.",
  "Total assets, four hundred thousand dollars.",
  "Now the other side. Current liabilities. Accounts payable, forty thousand. Wages owed, ten thousand. Total current liabilities, fifty thousand dollars.",
  "Long-term liabilities. A bank loan, one hundred fifty thousand dollars.",
  "So total liabilities are two hundred thousand dollars.",
  "And equity. Four hundred thousand in assets, minus two hundred thousand in liabilities, leaves two hundred thousand dollars of owners' equity. Liabilities of two hundred, plus equity of two hundred, equals four hundred. It balances.",
  "Part of that equity is something called retained earnings. That is the total profit the company has earned over its life and kept, rather than paying out to the owners. Say Skyline has one hundred twenty thousand dollars of retained earnings. The rest of the equity is the money the owners originally put in. Remember retained earnings. It shows up in the Altman Z-score later."
 ],
 "equation": [
  "And here is the rule that ties it all together. Assets equal liabilities plus equity. Always. Every time. It is called the accounting equation, and the balance sheet is called a balance sheet because both sides must balance.",
  "You already know this from real life. Say you own an airplane worth one hundred thousand dollars, and you owe the bank forty thousand on it. Your equity in that airplane is sixty thousand. Assets, one hundred thousand. Liabilities, forty thousand. Equity, sixty thousand. Forty plus sixty equals one hundred. That is the whole idea. A company is just a bigger version of that airplane."
 ],
 "accrual": [
  "Now, depreciation. When a company buys an aircraft or a machine, it does not count the entire cost as an expense in the first year. The thing will last for years. So the cost is spread out over its useful life. Each year, a slice of the cost is recorded as an expense. That slice is depreciation. It is not a cash payment. It is an accounting way of saying, this equipment is wearing out, and we are using it up.",
  "You know this as wear and tear on an airframe. The accountants just put a number on it.",
  "One important idea here is called accrual accounting. Under accrual accounting, a company records revenue when it is earned, and records expenses when they are incurred. Not when the cash moves. So if Skyline installs a new autopilot in December and sends the bill, that sale counts in December, even if the customer pays in January. This gives a truer picture of the business's performance in a given period.",
  "But it also means the income statement can tell a different story than the bank account. Which brings us to the third statement."
 ],
 "income": [
  "Next, the income statement.",
  "If the balance sheet is a photograph, the income statement is a movie. It covers a period of time, like a quarter or a year. And it answers the question, did we make money?",
  "It reads from top to bottom like a waterfall.",
  "At the top is revenue. Sometimes called sales. It is the money earned from customers. Then you subtract the cost of goods sold, often written as C-O-G-S. That is the direct cost of making or buying what you sold. For Skyline, it is what they paid for the radios and displays, plus the direct labor to install them. Revenue minus cost of goods sold gives you gross profit.",
  "Then you subtract operating expenses. Salaries for the office, rent, marketing, utilities, and depreciation. What is left is called operating income. You will also see it called E-B-I-T, which stands for earnings before interest and taxes.",
  "Then you subtract interest, the cost of borrowing money. And then taxes. And the bottom line, what is left, is net income. Profit.",
  "That is where the phrase bottom line comes from.",
  "Here is Skyline's year. Revenue, six hundred thousand dollars. Cost of goods sold, three hundred sixty thousand. So gross profit is two hundred forty thousand dollars. Operating expenses, one hundred fifty thousand. So operating income, or E-B-I-T, is ninety thousand. Interest expense, ten thousand. That leaves eighty thousand before taxes. Taxes, twenty thousand. Net income, sixty thousand dollars."
 ],
 "cashflow": [
  "The statement of cash flows.",
  "Profit and cash are not the same thing. You have seen this in real life. A company can show a nice profit and still run out of money, if customers are slow to pay. In aviation terms, a company can look great on paper and still have an empty fuel tank. The cash flow statement tells you whether there is actually fuel in the tank.",
  "It has three sections.",
  "Operating activities. Cash from the normal running of the business. It starts with net income, adjusts for things like depreciation, which was an expense that did not use any cash, and adjusts for changes in receivables, inventory, and payables.",
  "Investing activities. Cash spent on or received from long-term assets. Buying equipment is cash out. Selling an old building is cash in.",
  "Financing activities. Cash from and to the people who fund the company. Borrowing money is cash in. Repaying a loan is cash out. Paying dividends or owner distributions is cash out.",
  "Here is Skyline's year. Operating. Net income was sixty thousand dollars. Add back twenty-five thousand of depreciation, because that expense did not use any cash. Then subtract ten thousand, because customers owe Skyline ten thousand more than last year. That sale counted as revenue, but the cash has not come in yet. So cash from operations is seventy-five thousand dollars.",
  "Investing. Skyline bought forty thousand dollars of new equipment. So investing is negative forty thousand.",
  "Financing. Skyline paid down twenty thousand of the bank loan, and paid five thousand to the owners. So financing is negative twenty-five thousand.",
  "Add them up. Seventy-five, minus forty, minus twenty-five, equals plus ten thousand. Skyline's cash grew by ten thousand dollars during the year.",
  "Notice that net income was sixty thousand, but cash only grew by ten thousand. That gap is exactly why you need all three statements. Each one shows something the others hide.",
  "Here is a simple way to remember how they connect. The income statement produces net income. Net income flows into retained earnings, on the balance sheet. And the cash flow statement explains why the cash balance on the balance sheet changed. They are three views of one business."
 ],
 "liquidity": [
  "Now we put the statements to work. This is ratio analysis. And it is the heart of your Business Development class, because your project is to analyze a real company from its annual report.",
  "Here is why ratios exist. A big number alone tells you very little. If I tell you a company earned one million dollars, is that good? If it has ten million in sales, that is okay. If it has two hundred million in sales, that is poor. If it took a billion dollars of assets to make it, that is weak. Ratios put numbers in proportion.",
  "They let you compare a company to its own past, to its competitors, and to its industry.",
  "It is like a pilot comparing fuel burn. Total gallons burned means little. Gallons per hour means a lot. A ratio is just gallons per hour for a business.",
  "There are four families of ratios. Liquidity, which asks, can the company pay its short-term bills? Leverage, which asks, how much does it rely on debt? Profitability, which asks, how well does it earn? And efficiency, which asks, how well does it use its assets? We will go through each, using Skyline's numbers.",
  "Let me pull Skyline's key numbers together so you can follow. Current assets, one hundred thousand. Current liabilities, fifty thousand. Cash, fifty thousand. Receivables, thirty thousand. Inventory, twenty thousand. Total assets, four hundred thousand. Total liabilities, two hundred thousand. Equity, two hundred thousand. Revenue, six hundred thousand. Cost of goods sold, three hundred sixty thousand.",
  "Operating income, ninety thousand. Interest, ten thousand. Net income, sixty thousand.",
  "Liquidity first.",
  "The current ratio. Current assets divided by current liabilities. Skyline: one hundred thousand divided by fifty thousand equals two. We say two point oh, or two to one. It means Skyline has two dollars of short-term resources for every dollar due within a year. Think of it as your reserve fuel. Above one means you can cover your short-term bills.",
  "Many analysts like to see somewhere around one and a half to two, but it varies a lot by industry. A grocery store can run lean. A manufacturer with slow-selling inventory needs more.",
  "The quick ratio, also called the acid-test ratio. It is stricter. It leaves out inventory, because inventory can take a long time to turn into cash. Quick assets are cash, short-term investments, and receivables. Skyline: cash fifty thousand plus receivables thirty thousand, which is eighty thousand, divided by fifty thousand, equals one point six.",
  "So even without selling a single radio off the shelf, Skyline can cover its short-term bills one and a half times over. Comfortable.",
  "Working capital is not a ratio, but it goes along with these. It is current assets minus current liabilities. Skyline: one hundred thousand minus fifty thousand, equals fifty thousand dollars. That is the cushion."
 ],
 "leverage": [
  "Next, leverage. How much does the company lean on borrowed money?",
  "Debt-to-equity. Total liabilities divided by total equity. Skyline: two hundred thousand divided by two hundred thousand equals one. For every dollar the owners have in the business, creditors have one dollar. Higher means more risk. In a bad year, the interest payments still have to be paid. In flying terms, high leverage is like flying with a thin fuel reserve. It works fine when the weather is good.",
  "It is dangerous when the weather turns. Some books define debt-to-equity using only interest-bearing debt, and some use all liabilities. Use whichever your professor or textbook uses.",
  "Times interest earned. Operating income divided by interest expense. Skyline: ninety thousand divided by ten thousand equals nine. Skyline earns nine times what it needs to cover its interest. That is a healthy margin. If this number falls close to one, the company is barely covering its interest, and that is a warning light."
 ],
 "profit": [
  "Profitability. How well does the business earn?",
  "Gross margin. Gross profit divided by revenue. Skyline: two hundred forty thousand divided by six hundred thousand, equals zero point four, or forty percent. Out of every dollar of sales, forty cents is left after paying for the product itself.",
  "Net profit margin. Net income divided by revenue. Skyline: sixty thousand divided by six hundred thousand, equals ten percent. Out of every sales dollar, ten cents ends up as profit.",
  "Return on assets, or R-O-A. Net income divided by total assets. Skyline: sixty thousand divided by four hundred thousand, equals fifteen percent. For every dollar of stuff the company owns, it earned fifteen cents. It tells you how well management is using what it has.",
  "Return on equity, or R-O-E. Net income divided by owners' equity. Skyline: sixty thousand divided by two hundred thousand, equals thirty percent. Now look at that. Return on assets is fifteen percent, but return on equity is thirty percent. Why the difference? Because Skyline borrowed. It used the bank's money, as well as the owners' money, to earn profit. When borrowing is working, it boosts the return to owners.",
  "That is called leverage, and it works in both directions. It boosts good years and magnifies bad ones."
 ],
 "efficiency": [
  "Last family. Efficiency.",
  "Asset turnover. Revenue divided by total assets. Skyline: six hundred thousand divided by four hundred thousand, equals one point five. Every dollar of assets generates a dollar fifty in sales. A higher number means the company is working its assets harder. An airline's aircraft are a good example. An airplane sitting at the gate earns nothing. An airplane flying all day earns money. Turnover measures that.",
  "Inventory turnover. Cost of goods sold divided by inventory. Skyline: three hundred sixty thousand divided by twenty thousand equals eighteen. Skyline sells through its entire inventory eighteen times a year, about every twenty days. Textbooks often use the average inventory over the year rather than the year-end figure, so watch for that. If this number drops over time, inventory is piling up.",
  "That is often a sign of trouble.",
  "Now, here is a good habit. The ratios are not separate facts. They are a story. Skyline has a decent current ratio, moderate debt, strong coverage of interest, a ten percent net margin, and good asset use. That is a healthy small company. When you do your project, you will write that kind of story about a real company, using the ratios as evidence. A list of ratios is not an analysis.",
  "An explanation of what they say about the business is.",
  "And one honest caution. Different books define some of these ratios slightly differently. If your Cengage textbook or your professor gives a formula, use theirs."
 ],
 "hv": [
  "Now two techniques that your course uses, horizontal analysis and vertical analysis. Both are simple.",
  "Horizontal analysis compares the same line across years. You are reading across the page, left to right. The formula is the change divided by the earlier year. This year minus last year, divided by last year.",
  "Say Skyline's revenue last year was five hundred thousand, and this year it is six hundred thousand. The change is one hundred thousand, divided by five hundred thousand, equals twenty percent growth. Net income went from forty thousand to sixty thousand. The change is twenty thousand, divided by forty thousand, equals fifty percent growth. Notice that profit grew faster than sales.",
  "That tells you the company got more efficient. That is a good sign, and it is the kind of observation your professor wants to see written in a sentence.",
  "Vertical analysis expresses every line as a percentage of one total. You are reading down the page. On the income statement, you divide each line by revenue. For Skyline, cost of goods sold is sixty percent of revenue. Gross profit is forty percent. Operating expenses are twenty-five percent. Net income is ten percent.",
  "Now you can compare Skyline to a competitor of any size, because everything is a percentage of sales.",
  "On the balance sheet, you divide each line by total assets. Skyline's cash is fifty thousand out of four hundred thousand, or twelve and a half percent. Equipment is seventy-five percent. Liabilities are fifty percent.",
  "Think of horizontal analysis as watching a trend, like tracking your altitude over time. Vertical analysis is like looking at the makeup of the cargo, what percent is what."
 ],
 "altman": [
  "Now, the Altman Z-score. This one sounds fancy, but the idea is simple.",
  "Edward Altman is a finance professor who studied companies that went bankrupt. He found that a handful of ratios, combined in a certain way, did a good job of flagging trouble. He turned those ratios into a single score. It is a sort of risk gauge for a company. Think of it as a warning annunciator panel. It does not tell you the exact failure. But it tells you when to pay attention.",
  "The original formula was built for publicly traded manufacturing companies. It has five ingredients. I will call them A through E.",
  "A is working capital divided by total assets. That measures short-term cushion.",
  "B is retained earnings divided by total assets. That measures how much of the company was built from its own accumulated profits. A young company has little. An old, steady company has plenty.",
  "C is operating income, E-B-I-T, divided by total assets. That is how productive the assets are.",
  "D is the market value of equity divided by total liabilities. In plain terms, how big is the cushion of owners' value, as priced by the stock market, sitting beneath the creditors?",
  "E is sales divided by total assets. That is asset turnover.",
  "Each is multiplied by a weight. A gets one point two. B gets one point four. C gets three point three. D gets point six. And E gets one point oh. Add them up. That is the score.",
  "Notice that C, the earning power of the assets, has the biggest weight. That makes sense. A company that earns well on its assets is rarely in trouble.",
  "Then you read the score. Above two point nine nine is the safe zone. Between one point eight one and two point nine nine is the grey zone, meaning, be careful. Below one point eight one is the distress zone, meaning, a higher chance of bankruptcy.",
  "Let's run Skyline. Pretend the market value of its equity is three hundred thousand.",
  "A, fifty thousand divided by four hundred thousand, is point one two five. Times one point two, equals point one five.",
  "B, retained earnings of one hundred twenty thousand divided by four hundred thousand, is point three. Times one point four, equals point four two.",
  "C, ninety thousand divided by four hundred thousand, is point two two five. Times three point three, equals about point seven four.",
  "D, three hundred thousand divided by two hundred thousand, is one point five. Times point six, equals point nine.",
  "E, six hundred thousand divided by four hundred thousand, is one point five. Times one, equals one point five.",
  "Add them. Point one five, plus point four two, plus point seven four, plus point nine, plus one point five. That is about three point seven one. Above two point nine nine. Safe zone.",
  "Two cautions. First, the weights I gave are for the original version. There are other versions for private companies and for non-manufacturers. They use different weights and different cutoffs. Your professor may use a different version, so go with the one in class. Second, a score is a screening tool, not a verdict. It tells you where to look harder. It does not replace judgment.",
  "And with your background, judgment is your strong suit."
 ],
 "tenk": [
  "Now the last piece for this section. The annual report, called the ten-K.",
  "The ten-K is the annual report that a publicly traded U.S. company files with the Securities and Exchange Commission, the S-E-C. It contains the audited financial statements, and a lot more. And your Business Development project is to analyze one. So this is how you read it.",
  "Think of the ten-K as a long pre-flight briefing. You do not read every page from front to back. You know where to look.",
  "You find it free on the S-E-C's website. Go to a site called EDGAR. Type in the company's name. Filter for the form type ten-K.",
  "Here is a tour of what is inside.",
  "Item one, is Business. This describes what the company actually does, its products, its customers, its competitors. Start here to understand the company.",
  "Item one A is Risk Factors. This is the company telling you everything that could go wrong. It can be long and a little repetitive, but it is gold. If you want to understand a company's real weak spots, read these.",
  "Item seven is called Management's Discussion and Analysis, or M-D-and-A. This is management explaining in plain words how the year went, why revenue rose or fell, and what they expect. It is the closest thing to a narrator for the numbers. If you only have time to read one section carefully, make it this one.",
  "Item eight is the Financial Statements. The balance sheet, income statement, cash flow statement, and the statement of equity. Followed by the notes to the financial statements. The notes are important. They explain how the accountants treated things like revenue, depreciation, and debt. Experienced readers often say that the footnotes are where the truth hides.",
  "There is also a letter from the independent auditor, saying whether the statements fairly present the company's results. Read it. If there is anything unusual in it, that is a red flag.",
  "A practical tip. The statements show at least two years, usually three for the income statement. That is what you need for horizontal analysis. So one ten-K gives you the trend. And you can calculate every ratio we just covered from the statements in Item eight.",
  "Another tip. Pick a company you find interesting. Maybe one from an industry you know. You will spend weeks on it, and curiosity makes the work much easier."
 ],
 "oppcost": [
  "First idea. Opportunity cost.",
  "The true cost of anything is what you give up to get it. Not the price tag. The best alternative you did not choose.",
  "Here is an example from your world. Suppose you own an airplane, and you decide to fly it for fun on a Saturday. The out-of-pocket cost is fuel, oil, and a little wear on the engine. Say three hundred dollars. But what if that same Saturday you could have flown a charter for a paying customer and earned one thousand dollars? Then the real cost of your fun flight is not three hundred dollars.",
  "It is three hundred in direct costs, plus the one thousand dollars you gave up. Thirteen hundred dollars. The thousand is the opportunity cost.",
  "Economists care about this because accounting only records money that moves. It does not record the money you could have earned but did not. Economists call the first kind explicit costs, and the second kind implicit costs. Accounting profit subtracts only explicit costs. Economic profit subtracts both.",
  "Here is a good example. A business owner earns an accounting profit of eighty thousand dollars a year running her own shop. But she could have earned one hundred thousand dollars a year working for someone else. Her economic profit is negative twenty thousand. She is, in economic terms, losing money by running her own business, even though the books show a profit.",
  "That is a hard, honest way to look at a business, and it is how this class will teach you to think.",
  "Remember, every decision has an opportunity cost, even when no money changes hands. Your time is the best example. This MBA has an opportunity cost, too. You are spending hours that could be spent on something else. The question is whether the benefit is worth it. You have already made that call. But now you have the language for it."
 ],
 "marginal": [
  "Second idea. Marginal analysis.",
  "The word marginal means extra. The next one. The additional unit. And the rule is simple. Keep doing more of something as long as the extra benefit is greater than the extra cost. Stop when the extra cost catches up with the extra benefit.",
  "Think about adding one more flight a day to a small charter operation. Not the average cost of a flight. The extra cost of that one additional flight. Say the extra fuel, pilot pay, and landing fees come to eight hundred dollars. And the extra revenue from that flight is eleven hundred. Extra benefit exceeds extra cost by three hundred. Do it.",
  "Now add another flight after that, but this time, you have to fly during a slow time, and the revenue is only seven hundred. Extra cost eight hundred, extra benefit seven hundred. Do not do it. You stop at the point where the marginal flight stops paying.",
  "Here is where people make mistakes. They look at averages instead of margins. Say the average cost per flight is one thousand dollars, including a share of the hangar rent and insurance. A flight that brings in nine hundred dollars looks like a loser, because nine hundred is less than the average of one thousand. But the hangar rent and insurance are due either way.",
  "If the extra cost of that flight is only six hundred, then it adds three hundred dollars to profit. Decisions should be made at the margin, not at the average.",
  "This is an engineer's idea, really. It is the derivative. The rate of change. You find the best point by looking at the slope, and you stop where the slope says it is no longer worth it. You are well equipped for this."
 ],
 "sunk": [
  "Third idea. Sunk costs.",
  "A sunk cost is money already spent that you cannot get back, no matter what you decide next. And the rule is, ignore it. It is the hardest rule in economics to follow, because it feels wrong.",
  "Here is an example. A company spends two million dollars developing a new software product. Midway, they learn the market has changed. The product will bring in only one million dollars in the future, and it will cost another one and a half million dollars to finish. Many managers say, we have already spent two million, we cannot quit now. That is the sunk cost trap. The two million is gone either way.",
  "The only question is, from today, do the future benefits, one million, exceed the future costs, one and a half million? They do not. So the right decision is to stop. Spending more only makes the loss bigger.",
  "A pilot's version is the get-there-itis trap. You have planned the trip, you have driven to the airport, you have told everyone you are coming. The weather is getting worse. The time and effort you already spent are sunk. They cannot influence whether it is safe to continue. Good pilots know that the right question is, looking forward from here, what is the best decision? The same thinking applies to a business."
 ],
 "costs": [
  "The related idea is fixed versus variable costs. Fixed costs do not change with how much you produce. Rent, insurance, a lease. Variable costs go up as output goes up. Fuel, materials, hourly labor. When fixed costs have already been paid, they are sunk for the purpose of the next decision, and only the variable costs matter at the margin."
 ],
 "sd": [
  "Fourth idea. Supply and demand.",
  "Demand is how much buyers want to buy at each possible price. The law of demand says, as price goes up, the quantity demanded goes down. People buy less of something when it costs more. Supply is how much sellers want to sell at each price. The law of supply says, as price goes up, the quantity supplied goes up. Sellers are happy to sell more when the price is higher.",
  "Draw it in your head. A graph with price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis. The demand curve slopes down. The supply curve slopes up. Where they cross is the equilibrium. That is the price at which the quantity buyers want exactly equals the quantity sellers offer. Nobody is stuck with unsold goods, and nobody is turned away.",
  "Here is an example. Think of the used market for light aircraft. Say there are a thousand buyers who would pay one hundred fifty thousand dollars for a particular model, but fewer if the price is higher. Say sellers will offer more airplanes at higher prices. The market finds a price, maybe one hundred twenty thousand, where the number of buyers equals the number of airplanes for sale.",
  "If the price is set too high, there are more airplanes for sale than buyers. That is a surplus. Airplanes sit on the ramp, and sellers cut prices. If the price is too low, there are more buyers than airplanes. That is a shortage. Buyers compete, and the price rises. The market pushes toward equilibrium.",
  "Now the part that trips people up. A change in price causes a movement along the curve. But other things shift the whole curve. These are the shifters.",
  "For demand, the shifters include consumer income, tastes, the prices of substitutes and complements, and the number of buyers. For example, if a new rule cuts the cost of getting a pilot's license, the demand for small aircraft may shift to the right. More people want them at every price. The price will rise, and the quantity will rise.",
  "For supply, the shifters include input costs, technology, and the number of sellers. If the price of aviation fuel falls, that shifts supply to the right, and the price of flights falls.",
  "The key phrase: a price change moves you along the curve. Anything else shifts the curve. If you remember only one thing from this section, remember that."
 ],
 "elasticity": [
  "Fifth idea. Elasticity.",
  "Elasticity measures how sensitive buyers are to price. The formula for price elasticity of demand is the percent change in quantity demanded, divided by the percent change in price. Because demand slopes down, the answer is negative, but most people drop the sign and look at the size.",
  "If the number is greater than one, demand is elastic. Buyers are sensitive. A price change leads to a larger percent change in quantity. If the number is less than one, demand is inelastic. Buyers are not very sensitive. If it is exactly one, it is called unit elastic.",
  "What makes demand elastic? Having lots of substitutes. A luxury. A big share of your budget. And lots of time to adjust. What makes it inelastic? Necessities, few substitutes, and a small share of the budget. Think of someone who must fly to a funeral tomorrow. That is an inelastic buyer. Last-minute business travelers are less price-sensitive than vacationers booking six months ahead.",
  "And that is exactly why airlines charge them different prices.",
  "Here is the useful connection, and it is a favorite exam question. Elasticity tells you what happens to total revenue when you change your price.",
  "Suppose you sell something at one hundred dollars, and you sell one thousand units. Revenue is one hundred thousand dollars. You raise the price ten percent, to one hundred ten.",
  "If demand is elastic, say elasticity of minus two, quantity drops twenty percent, to eight hundred units. Revenue is one hundred ten times eight hundred, or eighty-eight thousand dollars. Revenue fell. So when demand is elastic, raising price lowers revenue, and cutting price raises revenue.",
  "If demand is inelastic, say elasticity of minus point four, quantity falls only four percent, to nine hundred sixty units. Revenue is one hundred ten times nine hundred sixty, or one hundred five thousand six hundred dollars. Revenue rose. So when demand is inelastic, raising price raises revenue.",
  "Here is a rule of thumb. Elastic, price and revenue move in opposite directions. Inelastic, they move in the same direction.",
  "One technical note. When you calculate percent changes, textbooks sometimes use the midpoint method, which divides by the average of the two values instead of the starting value. That way, you get the same answer going up or down. If your book uses it, use it."
 ],
 "structures": [
  "Sixth idea. Market structures and pricing.",
  "How much pricing power a company has depends on how many competitors it faces and how different its product is. Economists sort industries into four basic types.",
  "First, perfect competition. Many sellers, all selling the identical product, and nobody can influence the price. Think of a farmer selling wheat. The price is set by the market. If the farmer charges a penny more, buyers go next door. In this world, a firm is a price taker. It can only choose how much to produce. Long term, profits in perfect competition get competed down to roughly zero economic profit.",
  "That means a normal return, after counting opportunity costs.",
  "Second, monopolistic competition. Many sellers, but each sells a slightly different product. Restaurants, hair salons, flight schools. Each has some pricing power because of its location, quality, or reputation. But if profits are high, new competitors enter, and that pressures profits back down.",
  "Third, oligopoly. A few large sellers, who watch each other closely. Airlines, wireless carriers, aircraft manufacturers. What one firm does affects the others, so each has to think about how the others will react. That makes oligopoly the home of game theory, which is coming up.",
  "Fourth, monopoly. One seller, with no close substitutes. The monopolist has the most pricing power, but not unlimited. It still faces a demand curve. If it raises the price too far, people buy less, or do without."
 ],
 "pricing": [
  "Now, basic pricing. The profit-maximizing rule for any firm with some pricing power is the marginal rule again. Produce up to the point where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. Marginal revenue is the extra revenue from selling one more unit. Marginal cost is the extra cost of producing one more unit. Below that point, one more unit adds more revenue than cost. Above that point, it does not.",
  "And here is a neat, practical connection to elasticity. A firm with pricing power should mark up its price more when demand is inelastic, and less when demand is elastic. A common form of the rule says that the markup, price minus marginal cost, divided by price, equals one divided by the elasticity, ignoring the minus sign.",
  "Example. Marginal cost is sixty dollars. Elasticity of demand is three. Then the markup over price is one third. So price minus sixty, divided by price, equals one third. Solve it, and the price is ninety dollars. Check. Ninety minus sixty is thirty. Thirty divided by ninety is one third. Yes.",
  "If buyers are less sensitive, say elasticity of two, the markup rises to one half. The price becomes one hundred twenty. If buyers are more sensitive, say elasticity of six, the markup drops to one sixth, and the price is seventy-two. See how it works. The less sensitive the customers, the higher the price. That is what the airlines and software companies do all day.",
  "A software company is a great example, because its marginal cost is almost zero. Once the software is written, one more copy costs close to nothing. So the big costs are the up-front development, which becomes sunk once spent. That is why software companies can price based on what customers will pay, not what the copy costs. And they often sell different versions at different prices, a basic one and a pro one.",
  "That is a way of charging price-sensitive customers less and less sensitive customers more."
 ],
 "game": [
  "Seventh idea. Game theory.",
  "Up until now, we have talked about decisions you make on your own. Game theory is the study of decisions where the best move depends on what other people do. Which is most of business. Pilots call it situational awareness. You have to think about the other party's options.",
  "A game has three parts. Players, strategies, and payoffs. Who is in the game, what moves they can make, and what each gets from each combination.",
  "The most famous game is the prisoner's dilemma. Let me give you a business version.",
  "Two airlines fly the same route. Each can charge a high fare or a low fare. If both charge high, each makes ten million dollars. If both charge low, each makes six million. But if one cuts its price while the other holds high, the cutter steals the customers, and makes fourteen million, while the airline that held its price makes only four million.",
  "Now think like Airline One. If Airline Two charges high, you can make ten by also charging high, or fourteen by cutting. So cutting is better. If Airline Two cuts, you can make four by holding high, or six by cutting. Again, cutting is better. No matter what the other airline does, cutting is better for you. That is called a dominant strategy. A move that is best no matter what the other side does.",
  "Airline Two thinks exactly the same way. So both cut. And they each end up with six million. But if they had both held high, they would each have had ten million. Each acting in its own self-interest produced a worse result for both. That is the dilemma. It is why price wars happen, and why they hurt everyone in the industry.",
  "The outcome where both cut is called a Nash equilibrium, named after John Nash. It is a situation where no player can do better by changing only their own move. Both cutting is stable. Neither can improve by raising prices alone, because they would just lose customers. Notice that a Nash equilibrium is not necessarily the best outcome. It is just stable.",
  "So how do firms escape the dilemma? One way is repetition. In real life, airlines compete again and again, every day. If you cut your price today, your rival can punish you tomorrow. When the game repeats, the threat of punishment can help sustain higher prices. Another way is a contract, or some commitment that changes the payoffs. But careful. Competitors who agree to fix prices are breaking antitrust laws.",
  "The legal ways to cooperate are limited, and a manager needs to know where the line is.",
  "The prisoner's dilemma shows up well beyond pricing. Arms races. Advertising wars, where each company spends heavily and the total market does not grow. Two partners each tempted to shirk. When you see two parties both worse off because each acted rationally, think prisoner's dilemma.",
  "Your course also covers bargaining in week six. The key idea there is that the outcome of a negotiation depends on your alternatives if the deal falls through. The better your next-best option, the stronger your position. You have done this in real negotiations. A pilot who has three job offers negotiates differently than one who has none. That is the whole idea."
 ],
 "asymmetric": [
  "Eighth idea. Asymmetric information.",
  "Asymmetric means not equal. Asymmetric information means one side of a deal knows more than the other. The seller of a used airplane knows its history. The buyer does not. The borrower knows how risky the project is. The bank does not. Information is power, and when it is lopsided, markets can go wrong.",
  "There are two classic problems.",
  "The first is adverse selection. That is a problem of hidden information before the deal. The classic example is the used car market, which economist George Akerlof called the market for lemons. A lemon is a bad car. Suppose half the used airplanes for sale are good, and half are lemons. The buyer cannot tell which is which, so the buyer offers an average price.",
  "But owners of good airplanes will not sell at an average price, because their airplane is worth more. So they pull out of the market. What is left is worse than average. Buyers realize that, and lower their offers. Then even more good sellers leave. The market can spiral down. That is adverse selection. The sellers who stay are the ones with the worst products.",
  "Insurance has the same problem. People who know they are high risk are the most likely to buy insurance. So the company's customers are riskier than average.",
  "The second problem is moral hazard. That is hidden action after the deal. Once someone is protected from the consequences of risk, they may take more risk. A person with full insurance on an airplane may be a little less careful about hangaring it. A manager spending someone else's money may be less careful than with his own.",
  "So how do you fix these problems? Mostly by getting information out into the open. Sellers can offer signals. A good seller of a used airplane can show complete maintenance logs, welcome a pre-purchase inspection by an independent mechanic, or offer a warranty. A seller with a lemon will not offer a warranty, because it would cost too much. So the warranty itself is a signal that the product is good.",
  "Buyers can screen, by asking for logs and checking the history. Insurers use deductibles, which make the insured person share the loss, and that reduces moral hazard.",
  "A pilot's version is the checkride and the medical certificate. The examiner and the doctor are screening mechanisms. They exist because the passengers cannot see what the pilot knows about himself."
 ],
 "incentives": [
  "Ninth and final idea. Incentives and incentive conflicts.",
  "This is the topic of your last week. It is about a simple and important truth. People respond to incentives. And what you pay people for is what you get. Which is not always what you want.",
  "The standard setup is called the principal-agent problem. The principal is the owner or boss. The agent is the employee or contractor who acts for the principal. The trouble is that the agent's goals are not identical to the principal's, and the principal cannot see everything the agent does. That is asymmetric information again. It is the moral hazard problem, inside a company.",
  "Here are examples. A salesperson paid purely on revenue may cut prices too deeply to close a deal, because it is not her profit that is cut. She is rewarded for sales, but the owner wants profit. A manager paid based on this quarter's results may skip maintenance and training that would pay off next year. A contractor paid cost-plus has little reason to hold costs down.",
  "In aviation, imagine a company that gives pilots a bonus for saving fuel. That sounds good. But if it pushes a pilot to carry less reserve fuel than prudent, the incentive has produced a dangerous result. So companies pair incentives with firm rules and oversight. You know this well. Safety is not for sale at any bonus.",
  "So how do you fix incentive conflicts? Align the agent's rewards with the principal's goals. Pay on profit rather than revenue. Give managers a share of long-term results through stock or deferred pay. Monitor what you can monitor. And design contracts that reward the outcomes you actually care about.",
  "A good approach is to look at the decision, figure out who is making it, find out what that person gains or loses, and change the rewards so that good decisions for the person are good decisions for the company.",
  "A final tip for this class. When you read a case in the textbook, ask the questions in this order. What decision needs to be made? Who makes it? What do they know? What are they rewarded for? That four-question checklist works for nearly every problem in the course, and it is the same way you would have run a company."
 ]
};});
